'''bloody''' (blud′i), ''a.'' [Early mod. E. also ''bloudy''; < ME. ''blody'', ''bludy'', ''blodi'', etc., < AS. ''blōdig'' (= OS. ''blōdag'' = OFries. ''blōdich'' = D. ''bloedig'' = OHG. ''bluotac'', MHG. ''bluotec'', G. ''blutig'' = Icel. ''blōdhigr'' = Sw. Dan. ''blodig''), < ''blōd'', blood: see ''blood'' and ''-y''<sup>1</sup>.] '''1.''' Of, of the nature of, or pertaining to blood; containing or composed of blood: as, a ''bloody'' stream; “''bloody'' drops,” ''Shak.'', As you Like it, iii. 5.—'''2†.''' Existing in the blood.
{{smaller block|Lust is but a ''bloody'' fire. ''Shak.'', M. W. of W., v. 5 (song).}}
'''3.''' Stained with blood; exhibiting signs or traces of blood: as, a ''bloody'' knife.—'''4.''' Of the color of blood; blood-red.
{{smaller block|Unwind your ''bloody'' flag. ''Shak.'', Hen. V., i. 2.}}
'''5.''' Cruel; murderous; given to the shedding of blood, or having a cruel, savage disposition.
{{smaller block|<poem>The boar, that ''bloody'' beast.</poem>
{{right|''Shak.'', Venus and Adonis, l. 999.}}
He was a ''bloudye'' man, and regarded not the life of her subjectes noe more then dogges. ''Spenser'', State of Ireland.}}
'''6.''' Attended with or committing bloodshed; marked by cruelty: as, a ''bloody'' battle.
{{smaller block|This Ireton was a stout rebell, and had ben very ''bloudy'' to the King’s party. ''Evelyn'', Diary, March 6, 1652.}}
'''7.''' Concerned with or portending bloodshed; sanguinary.
{{smaller block|<poem>No magicke arts hereof had any might,
Nor ''bloody'' wordes of bold Enchaunters call.</poem>
{{right|''Spenser'', F. Q., I. vii. 35.}}}}

