'''8.''' In low language: (''a'') Excessive; atrocious; heinous: as, he’s a ''bloody'' fool, or a ''bloody'' rascal. (''b'') Used as an intensive expletive, especially in negative expressions: as, there wasn’t a ''bloody'' soul there.—'''Bloody bill.''' {{smaller|Same as ''force-bill'' (which see, under ''force'').}}—'''Bloody bread, blood on bread, blood of the host,''' {{smaller|an appearance resembling drops of blood which sometimes occurs upon bread and other starchy substances. The red pigment is a product of either of two microscopic fungi growing in the substance discolored. One of them is ''Micrococcus prodigiosus'', belonging to the bacteria, and the other ''Saccharomyces glutinus'', one of the yeast fungi.}}—'''Bloody chasm.''' {{smaller|See ''chasm''.}}—'''Bloody flux,''' {{smaller|dysentery.}}—'''Bloody hand.''' {{smaller|(''a'') A hand stained with the blood of a deer, which, in the old forest laws of England, was sufficient evidence of a man’s trespass against venison in the forest. (''b'') Same as ''badge of Ulster''. See ''badge''<sup>1</sup>.}}—'''Bloody murrain.''' {{smaller|Same as ''symptomatic anthrax'' (which see, under ''anthrax'').}}—'''Bloody shirt.''' {{smaller|See ''shirt''.}}—'''Bloody statute,''' {{smaller|a name by which the English statute of 1539, the Act of the Six Articles, is sometimes referred to. See ''the Six Articles'', under ''article''.}} ='''Syn. 6.''' {{smaller|See ''sanguinary''.}}

