'''bloody''' (blud′i), ''v. t.''; pret. and pp. ''bloodied'', ppr. ''bloodying''. [< ''bloody'', ''a''. Cf. AS. ''geblōdegian'' (= OHG. ''bluotagōn'', ''bluotegōn''), < ''blōdig'', bloody.] To stain with blood.
{{smaller block|With my own wounds I’ll ''bloody'' my own sword.
{{right|''Beau. and Fl.'', Philaster, iv. 4.}}}}

'''bloody''' (blud′i), ''adv.'' [< ''bloody'', ''a''.] Very; exceedingly; desperately: as, “''bloody'' drunk,” ''Dryden'', Prol. to Southerne’s Disappointment. [Vulgar.]
{{smaller block|“Are you not sick, my dear?” . . . “''Bloody'' sick.”}}

